---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import UiLabel from "../../components/UiLabel.astro";
import HelpCard from "../../components/HelpCard.astro";

// Issue #1106, guide 1: getting started on iPhone, Android, and the
// browser — the account, the first profile, and the lock gate. Every
// screen name and button label is quoted from the app's own ARB strings
// through <UiLabel> (an unknown key fails the build), the lock-gate and
// privacy explanations reuse the in-app help cards, and every factual
// claim carries a `<!-- cite: ... -->` ledger entry checked by
// `site/scripts/check-claims.mjs`.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install lunarlog on iPhone or Android, or open it in a browser; create the account, set up your first profile, and learn how the lock screen protects what you log."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Getting started</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      From install to your first logged day, on any device your family
      uses. The example here follows Sam, who is setting lunarlog up for
      herself — the <a href="/guides/inviting-someone/">inviting guide</a>
      picks the story up from there, in both directions.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Install, or open the browser version</h2>
      <p>
        lunarlog runs on iPhone, on Android, and in a browser. The phone
        apps are the full experience, including the lock screen and the
        optional health-app connections; the
        <a href="/guides/browser-version/">browser version</a> is a real
        lunarlog too, with its own storage trade-offs, explained on that
        page.
      </p>
      <ol class="doc__steps">
        <li>
          On a phone, install lunarlog from the App Store or Google Play.
          In a browser, go to <a
            href="https://app.lunarlog.app"
            rel="noopener">app.lunarlog.app</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open the app. On a phone, everything you log is protected by the
          device's own screen lock from the very first launch — the lock
          screen appears before the app's contents, and stays between your
          data and the world every time after.
          <!-- cite: lib/startup/gate/local_auth_gate.dart (requiresUnlock is always true on native); lib/gate_controller.dart -->
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p id="browser-first-steps">
        In a browser the order is the other way round. The browser version
        keeps nothing on the computer, so there is nothing to set up until
        you are signed in: create the account or sign in first — with an
        email and password, Google, or Apple — and then choose
        <UiLabel key="profilePickerEmptyAddAction" /> on the page that
        says <UiLabel key="profilePickerEmptyTitle" />. The rest of this
        guide follows the phone apps.
        <!-- cite: webapp/src/pages/SignedOutHome.tsx (signed out, the home offers sign-in and account creation only); webapp/src/lib/auth.ts (password, Google and Apple sign-in); webapp/src/pages/TodayPage.tsx (the empty home's Add profile link); PRIVACY.md#6-security-protections (nothing at rest in the browser) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Set up the first profile</h2>
      <p>
        A profile is one person's cycle log — its own name, history, and
        estimates. Sam's first profile is her own.
      </p>
      <ol class="doc__steps">
        <li>
          The first screen asks <UiLabel key="firstRunWhoLabel" />. Sam
          picks <UiLabel key="firstRunWhoMe" /> — the
          <UiLabel key="firstRunWhoSomeone" /> path is the same flow with
          the minor flag available, for a profile someone else will log
          for, at least for now.
          <!-- cite: lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart (first-run "who is this profile for" step) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Enter the person's name under <UiLabel key="firstRunNameLabel" />
          — a first name or nickname is normal; lunarlog never asks for
          more.
          <!-- cite: lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart (name field) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          A few optional questions follow — when the last period started,
          typical cycle and period lengths — and every one of them can be
          skipped: <UiLabel key="firstRunCycleCaption" />. Answers sharpen
          the early estimates; skipping costs nothing.
          <!-- cite: lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart (optional cycle questions, #216) -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Choose the <UiLabel key="firstRunCareModeLabel" /> when it is
          offered. Standard works for most adults; the life-stage modes
          change wording and reminders, not what is stored — and the mode
          can be changed any time from
          <UiLabel key="editProfileAction" />.
          <!-- cite: lib/domain/care_modes.dart (modes reframe copy, never data); lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart -->
        </li>
        <li>
          Tap <UiLabel key="firstRunCreateButton" />. That is the whole
          setup — logging takes one tap from here, and the
          <a href="/guides/logging-a-day/">logging guide</a> walks through
          it.
          <!-- cite: lib/ui/profiles/first_run_screen.dart (final create step) -->
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The account — now or later</h2>
      <p>
        You can log for weeks without an account: everything stays on the
        device. The account is what makes lunarlog more than a private
        notebook — it syncs a profile across your own devices and carries
        invitations to the other guardians in the household.
        <!-- cite: docs/product/positioning.md (the family-collaboration story); PRIVACY.md#1-core-principles -->
      </p>
      <p>
        When you are ready, create it from
        <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" />: <UiLabel
          key="accountSignInCreateAccountAction"
        /> asks for an <UiLabel key="accountSignInEmailLabel" /> and a
        <UiLabel key="accountSignInPasswordLabel" />, or you can sign in
        with Google on iPhone and Android, or with Apple on iPhone.
        <!-- cite: lib/startup/supabase_bootstrap.dart (email/password, Google on iOS+Android, Apple on iOS, passwordless email); lib/config.dart (feature defines) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        Signing in uploads this device's data only after your explicit
        consent — the app asks first. From then on, profiles and day
        entries sync between the household's signed-in devices.
        <!-- cite: lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart (syncWhat card); PRIVACY.md#2-information-we-collect-and-process -->
      </p>
      <HelpCard id="privacy-at-a-glance" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The lock screen</h2>
      <p>
        On a phone, the app's contents stay behind the device's screen
        lock. Reopen lunarlog and you will see
        <UiLabel key="gateLockScreenTitle" /> with a single
        <UiLabel key="gateLockScreenUnlockButton" /> step:
        <UiLabel key="gateLockScreenProtectedBody" />.
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/gate/lock_screen.dart; lib/startup/gate/local_auth_gate.dart -->
      </p>
      <p>
        If the phone has no screen lock at all, the app says so and offers
        to open the device settings: lunarlog leans on the protection the
        operating system already provides, so it asks you to add a
        passcode, PIN, pattern, or biometric lock rather than invent its
        own.
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/gate/lock_screen.dart (no-credential state); docs/ops/ios-export-compliance.md (OS at-rest protection posture) -->
      </p>
      <p>
        Two refinements live in <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" /> under
        <UiLabel key="settingsSectionPrivacySecurity" />: an optional
        in-app PIN on top of the device credential, and
        <UiLabel key="settingsRelockTitle" /> — how long the app waits
        before locking itself again when you step away.
        <!-- cite: lib/ui/gate/pin_settings_screen.dart (#271 optional PIN layer); lib/gate_controller.dart (inactivity relock) -->
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/logging-a-day/">Logging a day</a> — flow,
          symptoms, tags, and notes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/household-setup/">Setting up a household</a> —
          more profiles, and the four guardian roles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/inviting-someone/">Inviting someone</a> — bring
          a parent, partner, or the profile's own subject in.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      These guides follow the app's own built-in explanations, which work
      offline: <UiLabel key="settingsHelpTitle" /> in
      <UiLabel key="settingsTitle" /> has the same cards this site reuses,
      with their sources and review dates.
      <!-- cite: lib/ui/settings/settings_screen.dart (help section); lib/domain/help/help_cards.dart -->
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
